SM(235,{"n":"Genesis 12.6-20","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 12.6-20.mp3","d":2759.9,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.36,"Verse 6. Abram passed through the land to the place of Shechem, as far as the Oak of Morah, and the Canaanites were then in the land."],[33.12,"As they would be until long after Joshua."],[38.56,"That's at least some of them."],[42.16,"But so the idea here is when God brings him there and says, okay, this is the place."],[49.01,"This is a place that's already populated."],[52.45,"It's not like, oh, look, here's this big empty piece of land."],[56.15,"For you to settle."],[57.35,"We went to the outskirts of everything."],[59.28,"There's already people there."],[60.48,"There's already a city there, Shechem."],[63.46,"There's already this oak there that is famous enough that you could refer to it as a landmark."],[69.16,"So this is a settled place with history that God brings him to."],[77.0,"Verse 7, then the Lord appeared to Abram and said, To your seed I will give this land."],[83.48,"And there Abram built an altar to the Lord who appeared to him."],[86.84,"Okay, so again, this is Yahweh appears to him."],[91.64,"So there's not a voice drifting down out of the sky."],[94.49,"Shows up and speaks to him in person."],[97.13,"Abram builds an altar there by that oak to worship him via sacrifice."],[101.21,"This is the first"],[109.01,"We read about building altars in Canaan."],[113.41,"I'll probably comment on this repeatedly as we go through the rest of Genesis."],[119.25,"But there are going to end up being 11 more of these altars that Abram and his descendants are going to construct in different places at different times."],[131.17,"So Abram, Isaac, Jacob, uh, are going to build these these altars."],[137.17,"There's going to be 12 total."],[138.69,"And they are going to be not coincidentally in the territory that's given to the 12 tribes later in the Torah."],[145.97,"So that each tribe would have one sort of historic altar spot, from their from their forefathers."],[155.43,"And we'll talk more later on in the Torah, later on in the Pentateuch, um, about that, because there's a tension here."],[165.32,"Because what we've got, oh, here's these twelve historic places where Yahweh, the God of Israel, is worshipped by their ancestors, but once they get back there at the end of the Torah, they're not supposed to use any of them."],[185.8,"Because somebody else used them for bad things."],[187.88,"Because they're supposed to, well, because they're supposed to. only do that in Jerusalem, once David takes it"],[195.36,"And before that, only in the tabernacle, which is semi-mobile."],[201.66,"And it's going to be a Shiloh for a good chunk of that time."],[206.24,"So there's a tension there."],[209.84,"And when these are used again, it's going to be like by Jeroboam, the wicked king of Israel, who's going to use the shrine in Bethel and the shrine in Dan for uh paganized worship of ostensibly of Yahweh, but he's going to make golden calves right there that are going to be actually being being worshipped."],[233.36,"Um yeah, nevertheless, Genesis does mark these places"],[241.81,"So um I think the best understanding of this in terms of the intent."],[249.3,"So back in the introduction of the Torah to the Pentateuch, we talked about, the scholars who want to chop this up into pieces."],[255.94,"And do, well, this is from this, and this is from that, and this is from I think we even mentioned last time that one of the problems with that is that you have to have someone who's a genius and an idiot at the same time."],[266.84,"They have to be like a literary genius to pull all this stuff together and make this thing, but they also have to be so stupid that they don't notice that they're contradicting themselves like verse to verse."],[276.56,"Yeah. So this creates a certain problem."],[281.6,"Like, oh wait, I said this in this verse and then like three verses later I said this that totally contradicts it."],[286.48,"Ah, nobody will notice."],[289.36,"I'm sure this won't cause years and years and years of debate."],[291.92,"Yes. I mean I'm chopping all this stuff up so I mean I could just take one of them out but nah."],[300.7,"Yeah. So so that's that's sort of the easy the easy cheap uh modernist response is just like, well, okay, well, there was this one tradition where everybody had their own altar, and then that's contradicted much later."],[314.18,"The priests, you know, were trying to make this power play and say, no, no, only we can do the sacrifices at the temple in Jerusalem, and you know, you need to stop using all those."],[323.23,"And then you say this part was written like 500 years after the rest of it, and da da da da."],[330.03,"Um I mean, it's easy, but that's um there's a bunch of problems with it in terms of what actually is portrayed as happening."],[343.79,"Because, for example, the same text that's supposedly doing that chicanery where the priests are trying to trick you into thinking there's only one place you're allowed to worship God, also talks about Jeroboam worshiping it in other places, like these places and stuff."],[357.39,"So you're like, well, again, they're doing a lousy job of convincing me that, you know, that' thiss other yeah, like stuff didn't happen."],[364.99,"So um it ends up it ends up not working"],[367.59,"Yeah. But um there is when you study ancient Near Eastern religion, there's a whole set of rituals that people don't talk about as much, uh, but which becomes important, it becomes especially obvious and important when you get into Deuteronomy and Joshua in terms of what they're doing."],[396.31,"Um but there was among ancient Near Eastern culture the erection of standing stones."],[405.67,"So something would happen in a particular place."],[409.43,"Some event From our modern perspective, some of these events are things that a modern person would call an event, like a battle that was won, or the founding of a city or something."],[423.23,"Some of these events are things that a modern person would say did not happen, like something that we would call a myth."],[429.81,"Here is where, you know, Gilgamesh slew the bull of heaven."],[434.23,"For example. Something like that."],[436.09,"More commonly than not, it was a mix of the two."],[440.03,"So it'd be like this is the place where the city was founded by this god killing this dragon."],[445.4,"Like it was kind of a uh in that way."],[449.64,"But that for an ancient person, there wasn't."],[453.04,"And so a large stone would be stood up, or a pile of stones would be made to mark that location."],[462.62,"And we see that explicitly being done, as I mentioned in Deuteronomy and Joshua, as they're entering the land."],[469.36,"The place where they cross the Jordan, the place where they they set up these piles of stones."],[474.16,"And it even says, What are you to do with this?"],[476.8,"Well, you show it to your sons and your son's sons, and you say, This is where God did this."],[484.13,"At this place in the world."],[486.39,"Because again, they weren't making this distinction."],[489.11,"There's a place in the world where God did something."],[494.79,"But when I say there was a ritual, the ritual wasn't setting up the stones."],[499.31,"The ritual was returning to that spot, retelling the story and anointing the stones."],[512.83,"They would come, they would tell the story, they would pour oil over the stones."],[532.91,"To these founder figures, is that they were the plan was that they would be used, regardless of how they ended up being used."],[542.07,"The plan was they would be used in that way."],[546.67,"That they would go to that place and say, This is the place where Yahweh, the God of Israel, appeared to our father Abram and told him that he would be given this land, that his seed would be given this land."],[562.33,"And that's why they're indicating the altar being there."],[567.82,"Not because they were still going to use it for sacrifice."],[571.66,"And by having one for each of the twelve tribes, this becomes a unifying thing."],[579.84,"God and big favorites, basically."],[581.86,"And in their view of history. they're all connecting themselves back to Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, to the same family, to these same people"],[589.55,"Rather than to the founders of their individual tribe or their individual clan within that tribe."],[595.15,"They're all looking back to the same forefathers."],[600.51,"Is there any thing that lets us know which altar was for which tribe?"],[607.47,"You have to just map it out."],[610.11,"Because in numbers, everyone's favorite book of the Pentateuch, it gives all the land allotments."],[617.71,"Okay Not just for each tribe."],[619.81,"This tribe gets this piece, this tribe gets that piece."],[622.61,"Yeah But even the clans within the tribes and their pieces."],[629.37,"So you have to kind of map it out."],[630.81,"And you also have to take into account that there would be one in each tribe's land if the tribes had gone and done what they were supposed to do."],[640.57,"Some of them settle in different places."],[643.61,"Looking at you, Dan, looking at you, Manasseh."],[649.77,"Yeah. So some of them don't go to the place where they're supposed to go."],[654.01,"But if they went to the place where they were supposed to go, they would have had."],[658.73,"Yeah. Yeah. So it's a question of intent that doesn't get followed through on."],[666.79,"Which is an important principle when looking at the relationship between. the Torah and the rest of the Old Testament"],[674.91,"Because this is another thing that if, as I am compelled to do, you watch documentaries about the Bible on TV and streaming services."],[685.39,"Which are horrible."],[687.81,"One of the things they like to do is they'll have some quote from the Torah."],[693.03,"Or just some vague reference to it."],[694.55,"Oh, they were only supposed to worship Yahweh."],[696.87,"But look, we found this archaeological evidence that they were actually worshiping all these pagan gods."],[702.87,"Which of course, if you read later the Old Testament, it says, yeah, they were worshiping all these pagan gods that they weren't supposed to be worshipping."],[710.31,"Yeah. Ta-da. Jeremiah's very aware."],[713.82,"But they tr.ied to pit those against each other"],[717.87,"And the rest of the Old Testament, is the story of how Israel did not keep the covenant."],[729.2,"And God was faithful to them anyway, even though they were not faithful to him."],[736.29,"And for us as Christians, the ultimate end of that faithfulness is the incarnation of Christ."],[744.66,"And the culmination of the plan and the salvation of the world, through the seed of Abraham."],[750.34,"So that all comes to a culmination."],[752.42,"But either way, even if you're Jewish, and not a Christian, the rest of the story of the Old Testament is about Israel disobeying."],[763.18,"And being judged for it."],[765.66,"But then even within that, God remaining faithful to them."],[771.9,"And remaining their God."],[775.02,"So yeah, so don't fall for that when you see that in the Old Testament documentary."],[779.18,"Like. I mean, it's not even clever."],[783.26,"Like it's not even interesting."],[786.62,"It's like if you're at all familiar with the Old Testament, you're like, yeah, duh."],[789.82,"They didn't do what they were supposed to do."],[792.62,"We know. That's like every page."],[794.86,"Yeah. Okay. So I'll make a note here too, because why not?"],[810.66,"We're clearly not in a hurry."],[813.86,"About seed. So there's an interesting thing that happens here with the word seed in terms of these promises with Abraham."],[824.58,"Seed, Zara in Hebrew, is a collective noun."],[830.42,"So it's like fish in English."],[834.82,"So if I point at a fish tank and I say, fish."],[841.66,"How do you know if I mean one or a whole bunch?"],[845.58,"Depends on how you're there."],[847.19,"You have to look."],[848.49,"Because the word can be used for either."],[851.31,"That's what I mean by collective noun."],[853.23,"Or if I point into the woods and say deer."],[857.57,"It could be one, it could be a whole bunch of them."],[859.75,"So that's how zora, that's how the word that gets translated seed is in Hebrew."],[865.12,"I could point, say seed, and there could be one seed, there could be a pile of seed."],[871.3,"We even use seed sometimes collectively in English."],[874.32,"Bird seed. We don't say bird seeds."],[879.22,"Um in Hebrew, you can't you can't tell which it is."],[885.1,"For sure. There are places when you're reading this, like Abram's story and Abraham's story in uh Hebrew where it's you can kind of tell contextually it's one or the other."],[902.49,"Where contextually, like I you your seed is referring to the nation that's gonna come from him."],[907.29,"Obviously, that's a plural idea."],[909.87,"And sometimes it seems like seed is referring to Isaac, for example."],[914.99,"And you're like, oh, okay, well, that's that's singular."],[918.43,"And there's a bunch of cases where it's like, could go either way."],[927.55,"So, uh, Sperma in Greek, which is the word for seed, is not a collective noun, usually."],[937.11,"So when you translate Hebrew to Greek, say to produce the Septuagint, right here, you have to make that decision."],[950.01,"You have to decide is this plural and use the Greek plural, or is this singular and use the Greek singular?"],[957.53,"So you have to make a judgment call doing the translation."],[962.65,"This is one of the most interesting places where that happens."],[967.13,"Because St. Paul in Galatians points out that it says seed singular in a particular place instead of plural."],[982.21,"Which is an argument you can only make in Greek."],[988.31,"He says, look, it's singular."],[991.35,"And anybody who was only reading it in Hebrew would be like, wait, what?"],[998.39,"Like, I mean, it could be singular."],[1001.43,"Could probably be plural."],[1002.81,"But St. Paul's like, no, no, no, it's singular, not plural."],[1005.93,"So it's talking about Christ, Abraham's descendant, singular."],[1012.54,"Not all Israelites."],[1016.7,"ites. Or all Judah, Judeans, Jews"],[1024.92,"So that is an interpretive issue."],[1028.7,"All the way through here."],[1031.24,"And for example, in this verse, it's very difficult to tell."],[1035.56,"I'll give this land to your seed."],[1039.48,"It's probably plural."],[1041.74,"It's probably plural."],[1043.74,"But we'll comment on this as we go, because the word seed is going to be used as these promises are reiterated and detailed."],[1051.34,"And it's important to remember that there are times where it's clearly a singular idea, where it's referring to a particular descendant of Abraham's, not just to everybody."],[1063.58,"And sometimes particularly to Isaac."],[1067.18,"But then even when it's referring particularly to Isaac, as St."],[1073.02,"Paul's going to point out in Romans 9, sorry, Calvinists, uh, just in case this episode gets split in two, which it probably will."],[1080.3,"Um, we can have one each."],[1082.22,"Uh in Romans 9, St."],[1084.3,"Paul points out it's always singular."],[1087.84,"It's Isaac, not Ishmael."],[1091.2,"It's Jacob, not Esau."],[1095.65,"That beyond just the person of Isaac, the singularity of the line. of Abraham's descendants that receives the promise continues all the way through"],[1109.13,"And that's part and parcel of St."],[1110.49,"Paul's argument that this culminates in one person."],[1115.15,"Is that there's always this singular level of the promise."],[1118.83,"And then also a collective one."],[1121.41,"Because Ishmael's not like completely cut off."],[1124.54,"We're gonna see Esau isn't completely cut off."],[1127.52,"But there's gonna be the line is still this singular singular line."],[1137.28,"Verse 8. So he moved from there to the mountain east of Bethel, and he pitched his tent with Bethel on the west and Ai on the east."],[1145.68,"So there's number two"],[1146.44,"And there he built an altar to the Lord and called on the name of the Lord."],[1155.16,"Then Abram journeyed going on still toward the south."],[1158.76,"So he keeps going south."],[1161.8,"The other thing to note here, aside from the second altar, is place names."],[1168.76,"I mentioned back in the introduction that you have some folks who want to take a very literalistic interpretation of Genesis and say every word Genesis was written by Moses, and not nothinghing is updated, nothing is changed, and is."],[1190.76,"is So this the first example."],[1196.4,"There's gonna be a more egregious one in a couple chapters, but this is the first example."],[1200.16,"Uh Jacob is the one who names that place Bethel."],[1206.64,"Abraham's unborn grandson."],[1210.48,"So place names are getting updated."],[1213.04,"And I is a city that was there and was called that at the time Joshua showed up."],[1220.32,"Not in Abraham's time."],[1222.96,"Like I said, there's gonna be an even crazier one."],[1225.5,"But so it's very common all through the Torah for place names to be updated, and sometimes it's it's explicit."],[1235.1,"It'll say, this city, which used to be called, where you can tell somebody has come in and updated it"],[1241.04,"Because nobody remembers the old name anymore."],[1243.68,"Or sometimes, like this, it's just the new name."],[1248.74,"So there's there's been updates, throughout the so Abraham is now in the land that God has promised to give to his descendants, but he's not able to settle."],[1264.35,"There's people there."],[1265.81,"And so he's living still nomadically within within the land, moving from place to place."],[1273.57,"And he has to do that in part, also, this is gonna become an issue as we go forward. because he has all these flocks and herds"],[1282.97,"If you keep them in one place for too long, they're going to overgraze."],[1287.93,"And then they're going to run out of food and it won't grow back properly."],[1290.65,"So you have to keep moving."],[1293.21,"And so he's sort of moving south within Palestine."],[1299.63,"Within the Levant."],[1304.19,"So verse 10."],[1305.23,"Now there was a famine in the land."],[1308.43,"And Abram went down to Egypt to dwell there, for the famine was severe in the land."],[1313.99,"Okay, so here's the beginning of one of those patterns."],[1318.17,"Why did Joseph bring his father and his brothers, everybody down to Egypt?"],[1325.97,"Because there's a famine."],[1327.59,"So this is an all of this has happened before, and all of this will happen again."],[1332.07,"This is um so we see in sort of this um Abram as sort of the fat the founding father."],[1345.08,"As the father of the Israelite people, uh recapitulate, is it the right word?"],[1354.04,"Because he's protocapitulate."],[1355.96,"Am I forming a new word?"],[1357.56,"Um he sort of images what's going to happen to his descendants, but in advance."],[1366.2,"In this sort of prototypical way"],[1369.26,"He becomes sort of the prototype for what's going to happen to his later descendants."],[1376.78,"And this relationship between Genesis and Exodus is very important to understanding the way that the, particularly the Gospels, but this is present in the rest of the New Testament too, but it's particularly present in the Gospels."],[1394.7,"The way they're going to talk about Christ fulfilling elements of the Old Testament."],[1404.94,"Because the primary way they're going to talk about that, and St."],[1408.14,"Matthew's Gospel is exhibit A of this, is going to be Christ, and here you can say recapitulating, because it's out of the fact, but fulfilling in a more full way, him fulfilling what happened to or with Israel, though often in a better way."],[1427.7,"So sometimes there's a contrast."],[1430.12,"He goes and spends 40 days and 40 nights in the wilderness, and there is tested the way Israel spent 40 years in the wilderness and was tested."],[1438.78,"But Christ passes the test and Israel mostly failed."],[1441.52,"That generation. Other than Joshua and Caleb got wiped out."],[1445.68,"Um, but also in other ways, so when uh Saint Matthew quotes the prophet saying, Out of Egypt I have called my son."],[1458.08,"And he's talking about Christ uh as an infant, returning with the Theotokos and Saint Joseph from Egypt after Herod dies."],[1469.91,"Um people will point at that and go, Oh, that's ridiculous."],[1473.45,"That's talking about Israel coming out of Egypt."],[1475.85,"It's like, yes."],[1479.21,"That's exactly what it's talking about."],[1481.67,"Because there's a famine."],[1483.91,"God brings people of Israel into Egypt to save them from the famine, to protect them."],[1490.72,"And then when that's over, he brings them out and returns them to the land."],[1494.8,"Same way. There's Herod slaughtering the innocent children."],[1502.1,"Connection to Pharaoh, too."],[1503.8,"But God sends an angel."],[1508.49,"And brings Christ and his family to Egypt, and then when the threat's gone, brings them back out and returns them right to the land."],[1519.61,"So that same pattern, is fulfilled literally, filled full."],[1523.8,"Filled over full in Christ."],[1527.69,"Same thing with this is why you don't have to argue about Parthenos and Alma and all this stuff."],[1533.85,"Does it say virgin?"],[1534.89,"Does it say young woman?"],[1537.05,"In Isaiah, it's talking about a young woman."],[1539.09,"And the Theotokos was a virgin."],[1544.51,"Isaiah is in the middle of the Syro Ephraimite War."],[1550.35,"Okay, which is a poorly named war because it was not the Syrians fighting the Ephraimites."],[1556.19,"It was the Syrians and the northern kingdom of Israel, of whom Ephraim was the main tribe, together coming after Judah."],[1565.31,"But still called the Syro Ephraimite War."],[1569.31,"So they're coming, they're attacking, they're laying siege to Jerusalem."],[1574.91,"King of Judah, asks Isaiah the prophet, what's going to happen?"],[1580.45,"You need to give us a word from the Lord, some kind of hope."],[1585.34,"And he says, This will be assigned to you."],[1587.5,"There's a young woman here, she's going to have a baby, and before that baby is old enough to know good and evil."],[1593.87,"That's literally what he says."],[1595.07,"Before he knows that he's at the age of wisdom."],[1597.97,"Before that happens, you'll be delivered from your enemies who are laying siege to the city."],[1604.05,"That's what Isaiah says."],[1605.94,"And then St."],[1606.74,"Matthew says, Hey, do you remember that?"],[1611.94,"Well, in that story, when that baby is born, and people see that baby born, they know, oh, very soon we're going to be delivered from our enemies."],[1624.57,"He says, in a much fuller way, right"],[1628.53,"When Jesus is born, and the shepherds see it, they know their deliverance, their salvation, is now close at hand."],[1642.05,"So you don't have to argue about that."],[1643.81,"You don't have to argue about Oma and Parthenos."],[1646.13,"It's a waste of time."],[1648.54,"Them saying they're not the same word doesn't disprove anything."],[1651.42,"You trying to prove they're the same word doesn't prove anything, because that's not what St."],[1654.7,"Matthew's doing. It's just a waste of time."],[1657.04,"It's this pattern idea."],[1659.25,"But this is another step of that."],[1662.43,"This is a previous step."],[1664.59,"So Abraham, in his life, we're gonna see there are gonna be these things, and Isaac and Jacob and Joseph."],[1672.63,"Uh, there are gonna be these patterns first sketched here in Genesis in their lives, that are then gonna be partially fulfilled in the rest of the Torah."],[1683.75,"And then as Christians, the gospels are gonna argue, find their final fulfillment in Jesus."],[1692.01,"So we're at the rough sketch stage."],[1694.78,"Yeah, this isn't the only part of this story that's a rough sketch, but we already have the beginnings of this."],[1700.22,"Oh, there's a famine in Canaan."],[1703.18,"We're gonna go right to Egypt to wait this out."],[1709.56,"Isn't there any way too?"],[1710.92,"Yeah, there is, yeah."],[1711.24,"Anyway Spoilers. Okay."],[1720.96,"Verse 11. So it came to pass when he was close to entering Egypt that he said to Sarai his wife, indeed I know you are a beautiful woman."],[1731.84,"Therefore, it will happen when the Egyptians see you, so they will say, This is his wife, and they will kill me, but they will let you live."],[1740.4,"Therefore, say you are my sister, that it may be well with me for your sake, that I may live because of you."],[1747.12,"Okay So they're getting close to Egypt."],[1753.78,"So we're going back to civilization."],[1758.94,"As we've seen already in our goodest civilization."],[1762.0,"Remember, Cain builds the first city not that many chapters ago."],[1767.28,"But so Abram gets a little worried."],[1770.7,"He looks at his wife, who's like 70, and says, You're a super good looking woman."],[1780.43,"I know what's gonna happen."],[1781.63,"You know how those Egyptians are."],[1783.69,"They're gonna see you, they're gonna want to take you for their wife."],[1788.47,"They're gonna say, Oh, well, we need to get rid of the husband."],[1792.31,"So they're gonna off me so that they can they can take you for a wife."],[1797.61,"So just say you're my sister, that way they won't feel like they have to kill me."],[1802.45,"So let's look at some problems with this, with this whole mode of thinking."],[1808.58,"So perhaps the most obvious one is, you know, we're gonna see that Abraham, and of course, St."],[1817.38,"Paul famously uses Abraham as sort of the benchmark of faithfulness to God."],[1823.27,"Loyalty to God, trust in God."],[1826.36,"And he's made one pretty big show of it."],[1828.86,"God tells him, Hey, get up and go, and I'm not even telling you where, and Abraham doesn't."],[1833.64,"So he's made one good show of it, but now, what happened to the whole trust God thing?"],[1841.52,"Now he thinks he's gonna get killed in Egypt just randomly, and he needs to look out for himself through his own lying and treachery."],[1850.22,"So that's not good."],[1852.23,"But there's a second piece here that's also important."],[1856.2,"Because uh he's done something about himself not getting killed, but he hasn't done anything about someone, say, having relations with his wife."],[1870.04,"Which you'd think would be something he would want to prevent."],[1876.25,"In fact, someone might value that more than their own life."],[1884.85,"Not Abram at this point, but someone might."],[1889.99,"Someone might. And so there is going to be one of the dynamics going on in this story is not only about Abram's trust in and faithfulness to God, but also how he treats Sarai slash Sarah."],[1910.55,"We'll see that play out."],[1912.47,"We'll talk more about her name when her name gets changed."],[1916.11,"Um, but how he treats her and relates to her, this is gonna become important with the whole episode with Hagar."],[1925.83,"This is gonna be an undercurrent here."],[1928.21,"And this isn't me giving some kind of weird modern feminist reading, to the Torah, because we've already seen, that there's there's been a bunch of starting with, you know, back in Genesis 1, uh, male and female humans are both human, equally so and equally created in the image of God."],[1950.64,"Which, as we mentioned in the exit world was a major thing."],[1953.38,"Aristotle had still not caught up with this in the fourth century BC, when he he said that basically women were deformed men."],[1964.54,"Um which he thought was because they had a lower body temperature."],[1969.54,"Um other things."],[1972.78,"Uh yeah, it goes into bizarre places when you read."],[1976.62,"One of the most amazing things about uh Aristotle on medicine and the natural world is that he has he constructs this system that all makes sense that all works perfectly together."],[1988.14,"It is this beautiful understanding of the world, except that it has no connection to reality."],[1994.63,"Like it is almost all wrong."],[1997.27,"Like just completely wrong, but internally consistent."],[2001.71,"Um he must have just gotten scorned by a cold."],[2004.31,"Yeah He just didn't get it."],[2008.79,"Yeah, yeah. I won't go into much detail, but he did not get how women's bodies work."],[2013.91,"We'll put it that way."],[2014.63,"Um theorizing Um but so I mean, and that's centuries and centuries and centuries later."],[2024.72,"Um, though it is, and it's considered Aristotle is considered to be a major breakthrough because if you compare that to the Hippocratic corpus, like Hippocrates, like the early medical writings, they didn't consider women to be human, they thought they were a different species."],[2039.52,"So Aristotle at least thought they were human, just deformed humans."],[2044.54,"Um so that was he was the feminist of his day."],[2049.72,"Uh many centuries after this."],[2051.66,"So the Bible is very much on a different page here, and you remember how in chat Genesis chapter 2, at the creation of Eve, the way the relationship is spoken of between man and woman there, the way the marital relationship is spoken of, that's laying groundwork that this is addressing."],[2069.7,"So when we see this, and later with Isaac and Rebecca, we see these marriages, we are to see them in the light of what we've already read in Genesis."],[2080.18,"So this isn't bringing in some weird modern feminist."],[2083.46,"This is in the context of the text."],[2088.46,"You should not be like, well, I guess someone's gonna sleep with my wife either way."],[2093.34,"I just don't want to get killed."],[2095.03,"This is not a good husbandly approach."],[2103.19,"Yeah, exactly. Yeah."],[2104.71,"Like Adam standing there watching while she ate the fruit and seeing what happens."],[2108.39,"Don't do that."],[2111.59,"Well, she looks okay."],[2113.27,"I guess I'll try it."],[2114.23,"You know. She didn't drop dead."],[2117.27,"Um, yeah. But so this is his plan."],[2126.15,"So verse 14, so it was when Abram came into Egypt, the Egyptians saw the woman, that she was very beautiful."],[2134.07,"Notice that language."],[2137.51,"Saw the woman."],[2139.75,"So it's going back to the language from Genesis 2."],[2145.29,"Where Eve's name, her name becomes Eve after the expulsion from Paradise."],[2150.93,"After she has Cain."],[2152.85,"Before that, her name was just woman."],[2155.89,"It was man and woman."],[2157.17,"So now Sarai is the woman."],[2159.57,"So it's the text is again calling us back to that."],[2163.67,"And the language is not that dissimilar from the language that was used when she was looking at the fruit, not apple."],[2173.59,"Yeah. She's looking at the fruit."],[2176.28,"She looked at it."],[2177.4,"She saw that it was beautiful to look at, that it looked like it would taste good."],[2182.82,"So they see her, they see the woman, Sarai, and see that she is very beautiful."],[2189.18,"At 70, so this is impressive."],[2196.86,"Verse 15. The princes of Pharaoh also saw her and commended her to Pharaoh."],[2202.46,"Then the woman was taken to Pharaoh's house."],[2205.26,"So Pharaoh gets word about it, decides to add another woman to his harem."],[2215.9,"Which obviously kings at this time in history had multiple, multiple wives and concubines."],[2224.3,"Concubine, by the way, is the very polite word for sex life."],[2229.98,"Because we don't want to say that in Victorian English."],[2233.42,"But that's what it is."],[2234.79,"Lots of those two."],[2236.49,"Um, but so she's being taken to become another wife."],[2242.81,"Verse 16. Now he treated Abram well for her sake and gave him sheep, oxen, donkeys, male and female servants, mules and camels."],[2252.41,"So Abram's like, oh, okay, he's got, you know, pretty good lifestyle going in Egypt."],[2258.91,"Because if they were brother and sister, this is like, hey, if Pharaoh's gonna marry your sister."],[2263.11,"Now you're an in-law."],[2265.51,"Get all this stuff."],[2267.43,"Don't read anything here about Abram being mad about it or anything."],[2270.87,"Uh deeply upset, wanting to correct the issue."],[2275.35,"Verse 17 But the Lord plagued Pharaoh and his house with great plagues because of Sarai Abram's wife."],[2288.83,"So this is interesting."],[2292.51,"Why is Yahweh, the God of Israel, doing this?"],[2295.95,"What did he say to Abram early in the chapter?"],[2299.87,"He who blesses you, I will bless."],[2301.95,"He who curses you, I will curse."],[2305.55,"So whether Abram is caring about it or is acting right about it or not, Pharaoh is doing this evil thing to Abram."],[2318.35,"And so God responds to protect Sarai, even though Abram isn't and should be."],[2330.59,"So God steps in to do this."],[2335.47,"Again, uh, one of the amazing things about many biblical figures is that we see growth over the course of their life as it's described."],[2353.8,"Um, we see growth and change."],[2356.4,"We see them learn and come to understand things."],[2359.12,"We see that unfold over their relationship with God."],[2362.64,"So the scriptures aren't scared to say negative things or to reflect right the sins of the people who are quote unquote heroes."],[2373.05,"Who are put forth as heroes and role models because part of the repentance and the learning is what is being modeled to us."],[2381.31,"Because God doesn't, God knows our frame, he remembers that we are dust, to quote this all."],[2387.37,"He knows we're not going to be always perfect all the time and always heroic and always virtuous and always doing what is right."],[2394.1,"But the life he wants from us is one of repentance when we do wrong, of transformation, of growth."],[2401.32,"Of drawing closer to him."],[2403.16,"And so the people we're shown in scripture to emulate are people who did that."],[2406.6,"who did that People."],[2407.56,"Absolutely So this is very early."],[2414.78,"I know I've been going on and on, and this is our second Bible study, but we're not that many verses in Abram's story."],[2421.02,"We're seeing where he's starting out."],[2424.38,"And we're being given this contrast."],[2426.94,"On one hand, in this big thing of leave everything you've ever known, go to parts unknown."],[2437.04,"Abram was faithful, believed in, and trusted God."],[2441.04,"When it came down to this very personal thing of his own life, he waffled it a little."],[2450.48,"And when it came to protecting and being faithful to and treating his wife the way he should have, he blew it."],[2458.61,"He blew it here."],[2460.05,"But this is the beginning."],[2461.81,"This is where he's starting out."],[2463.33,"We're going to see this continue to unfold."],[2465.41,"But so God, as you mentioned, what does it say he does?"],[2469.81,"It doesn't say, oh, he cursed Pharaoh or he plagued."],[2475.27,"He sends plagues on Egypt over the way Pharaoh has treated the woman Sarah, his wife."],[2486.17,"You see Israel described as being the bride of God."],[2491.63,"As being the wife of God later."],[2493.57,"So again, we're getting the outlines of the pattern here."],[2497.93,"So after these plagues, as we mentioned, these plagues, this is the pattern, this is the sort of pre-pattern, establishing these broader patterns that are going to play out in the rest of the Torah, finally going to find their fullness in the person of Christ."],[2515.78,"After receiving all of these plagues upon Pharaoh, Pharaoh comes to Abraham."],[2525.86,"So Pharaoh called Abram and said, What is this you have done to me?"],[2530.18,"Why did you not tell me she was your wife?"],[2532.26,"Why did you say she is my sister?"],[2534.58,"I might have taken her as my wife."],[2536.42,"Now, therefore, here is your wife."],[2538.1,"Take her and go your way."],[2541.02,"So this makes it clear that by I might have taken her as my wife."],[2546.94,"This is talking about consummating the marriage at this point in history and all through the ancient world and even well into the medieval period."],[2556.06,"Uh the moment at which a marriage became a marriage was actually the consummation, the sexual consummation uh of that marriage."],[2565.74,"So he's saying here, he hadn't done that, but he's saying, I might have done that."],[2570.14,"And in Pharaoh's mind, then the plagues would have been worse."],[2574.29,"The accursedness would have been worse because he would have been committing adultery uh with another man's wife."],[2581.65,"Uh, and so he has to sort of correct and challenge and call Abram to repentance."],[2586.59,"But this is also with Pharaoh here."],[2589.81,"This Pharaoh, at least, unlike the Pharaoh of the Exodus, is able to look at what's going on, is able to look at the plagues, is able to see that something is wrong, and figure out what it is, and then try to correct it and try to make it right."],[2603.89,"This is the Pharaoh's job as far as the Egyptians are concerned."],[2607.34,"It's to maintain Ma'at, to maintain justice, everything in its place, everything in good order, everything functioning properly."],[2614.54,"So if this Pharaoh is able to make that assessment and act correctly, uh, and his sense of ma'at, his sense of justice is in line with God's sense of justice, which is why he's able to do this accurately."],[2629.54,"His over against Abram's."],[2632.0,"Over against Abram's in this case, he's being corrected by Pharaoh."],[2638.96,"But this is sort of then an anti-type for the Pharaoh of the Exodus, who, when even though he receives the plagues, is sort of unwilling to uh act as a righteous and just king, uh unwilling, unable, and refuses to."],[2657.6,"And so he says, Go your own way."],[2660.32,"The last verse here of the chapter."],[2662.24,"So Pharaoh commanded his men concerning Abram, and they sent him away with his wife and all he had."],[2667.2,"He keeps all of those things he got from Pharaoh when Pharaoh was treating him well for the sake of uh Sarai."],[2674.2,"And this, of course, again is part of that pattern from the Exodus, where his descendants, the Israelites, are going to pillage the Egyptians, sort of on their way out."],[2686.36,"So he leaves not only with all he had when he came, but with uh more beside uh after the plagues."],[2694.04,"So that pattern is complete."],[2696.2,"We see uh here, again, as I said, Abraham Abram at the beginning of his journey, the beginning of his life, the beginning of his growth, the beginning of his faithfulness uh to God."],[2709.24,"And we're gonna see now in the rest of Abram's story as he becomes Abraham, how uh this is going to ultimately play out and how he's going to learn and grow and change through his ongoing walking with God."],[2727.68,"So that's where we'll stop for this week."],[2730.16,"And next week we'll come back and pick up in chapter 13, verse 1."],[2734.8,"Thank you, everybody."],[2737.52,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[2744.88,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientFaith.com."],[2750.24,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.36,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 12:6-7: Shechem, the oak, first altar","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.6: Abram passes through to <b>Shechem</b>, the <b>Oak of Moreh</b>; <b>\"Canaanites were then in the land\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as they would be <b>until long after Joshua</b> - at least some of them</li><li class=\"d0\">God brings him to a place <b>already populated</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"a big empty piece of land\" - not outskirts of everything</li><li class=\"d1\">already a <b>city, Shechem</b>; an <b>oak famous enough to be a landmark</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>settled place with history</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v.7: <b>Yahweh appears</b> to him - \"To your <b>seed</b> I will give this land\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no voice drifting down from the sky - <b>shows up, speaks in person</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Abram builds an <b>altar</b> by the oak - worship via <b>sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>first altar</b> we read of in Canaan</li></ul>"},{"t":101.21,"lv":3,"h":"The twelve altars","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11 more</b> altars to come - Abram, Isaac, Jacob build them in different places - <b>12 total</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not coincidentally in the territory given to the <b>12 tribes</b> later in the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">each tribe gets <b>one historic altar spot</b> from its forefathers</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>tension</b> (more later in the Pentateuch)</li><li class=\"d1\">12 historic places where Yahweh was worshipped by the ancestors</li><li class=\"d1\">but at the end of the Torah they're <b>not to use any of them</b> - \"somebody else used them for bad things\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sacrifice only in <b>Jerusalem</b> once David takes it; before that the <b>tabernacle</b>, semi-mobile, at <b>Shiloh</b> a good chunk of the time</li><li class=\"d0\">When reused: <b>Jeroboam</b>, wicked king of Israel</li><li class=\"d1\">shrines at <b>Bethel</b> and <b>Dan</b> - paganized worship of \"ostensibly\" Yahweh, <b>golden calves</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Nevertheless Genesis <b>marks these places</b></li></ul>"},{"t":241.81,"lv":2,"h":"The \"cheap modernist\" answer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Back in the Pentateuch intro: scholars who <b>chop it into pieces</b> - \"this is from this, this is from that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">problem: redactor must be a <b>literary genius and an idiot</b> at once</li><li class=\"d1\">pulls it all together, yet doesn't notice <b>contradicting himself verse to verse</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"nobody will notice. I'm sure this won't cause years of debate\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Cheap answer: one tradition where <b>everybody had their own altar</b>, contradicted much later</li><li class=\"d1\">priests' <b>power play</b>: \"only we can sacrifice at the temple in Jerusalem\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"written 500 years after the rest\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems</li><li class=\"d1\">same text supposedly tricking you into one place <b>also tells of Jeroboam</b> worshipping elsewhere</li><li class=\"d1\">\"lousy job of convincing me\" → <b>doesn't work</b></li></ul>"},{"t":367.59,"lv":2,"h":"Standing stones in the ancient Near East","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ancient Near Eastern ritual people talk about less - obvious in <b>Deuteronomy and Joshua</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Erecting standing stones</b> where something happened</li><li class=\"d1\">modern view: some \"events\" (a battle won, city founded), some we'd call <b>myth</b> - \"Gilgamesh slew the bull of heaven\"</li><li class=\"d1\">usually <b>a mix</b>: \"place where the city was founded by this god killing this dragon\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient person: <b>no such distinction</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>large stone or pile of stones</b> marks the spot</li><li class=\"d1\">Joshua: where they <b>crossed the Jordan</b>, piles set up</li><li class=\"d1\">show your <b>sons and sons' sons</b>: \"this is where God did this\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The ritual = not setting up the stones</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>return to the spot, retell the story, anoint the stones</b> - pour <b>oil</b> over them</li><li class=\"d0\">So the altars: <b>the plan all along</b> was to use them this way, whatever happened</li><li class=\"d1\">\"here <b>Yahweh appeared to our father Abram</b>, promised the land to his seed\"</li><li class=\"d1\">altar there not for <b>sacrifice</b> any more - a <b>marker</b></li><li class=\"d0\">One per tribe = <b>unifying</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all connect back to <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b> - same family</li><li class=\"d1\">not to founders of their own tribe or clan</li></ul>"},{"t":600.51,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: which altar belongs to which tribe?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: anything that tells us which altar was for which tribe?</li><li class=\"d0\">Have to <b>map it out</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Numbers</b> - \"everyone's favorite book\" - all the <b>land allotments</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just per tribe: <b>clans within tribes</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\">Assumes each tribe settled where it was supposed to</li><li class=\"d1\">some didn't - \"looking at you, <b>Dan</b>, looking at you, <b>Manasseh</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So it's a question of <b>intent not followed through</b></li></ul>"},{"t":666.79,"lv":2,"h":"Intent vs. the TV documentaries","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Important principle: <b>Torah vs. rest of the Old Testament</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Bible <b>documentaries</b> - \"horrible\", but he watches them</li><li class=\"d1\">vague quote from Torah: \"only supposed to worship <b>Yahweh</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"but look, <b>archaeological evidence</b> they worshipped pagan gods\" - ta-da</li><li class=\"d1\">the OT <b>says so itself</b>; <b>Jeremiah</b> very aware</li><li class=\"d0\">Rest of the OT = story of how <b>Israel did not keep the covenant</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God faithful anyway</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for Christians the end of that faithfulness = <b>incarnation</b>, salvation through the <b>seed of Abraham</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even for Jews: Israel <b>disobeying, judged</b>, God <b>remaining their God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Don't fall for it\" - not even clever: \"yeah, duh... that's <b>every page</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":794.86,"lv":2,"h":"Aside: \"seed\" (<i>zera</i>)","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew <b><i>zera</i> = collective noun</b>, like <b>fish</b> or <b>deer</b></li><li class=\"d1\">point at a fish tank: one or many? - have to <b>look</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we do it in English: <b>\"bird seed\"</b>, not \"bird seeds\"</li><li class=\"d0\">In Hebrew can't tell for sure</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes context: \"your seed\" = the <b>nation</b> from him (plural)</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes seems to be <b>Isaac</b> (singular)</li><li class=\"d1\">many cases <b>could go either way</b></li></ul>"},{"t":927.55,"lv":3,"h":"<i>Sperma</i> and Paul in Galatians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Greek <b><i>sperma</i></b> usually <b>not collective</b></li><li class=\"d1\">translating into Greek (<b>Septuagint</b>) → must choose <b>plural or singular</b> - judgment call</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Galatians</b>: Paul points out <b>\"seed\" singular</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"an argument you can only make in Greek\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew-only reader: \"wait, what?\" - could be either</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: no - <b>Christ</b>, Abraham's descendant, <b>not all Israelites / Judeans</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Interpretive issue all the way through</li><li class=\"d1\">this verse: \"I'll give this land to your seed\" - <b>probably plural</b></li><li class=\"d1\">will comment as it recurs; sometimes <b>clearly singular</b>, one descendant - particularly <b>Isaac</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1067.18,"lv":3,"h":"Romans 9: the singular line","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Even when it's Isaac, Paul in <b>Romans 9</b> - \"sorry, <b>Calvinists</b>\" (joking, in case the episode gets split in two)</li><li class=\"d1\">always singular: <b>Isaac, not Ishmael</b>; <b>Jacob, not Esau</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Beyond Isaac: the <b>singularity of the line</b> carries all the way through</li><li class=\"d0\">Part of Paul's argument: culminates in <b>one person</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Singular level of the promise <b>and</b> a collective one</li><li class=\"d1\">Ishmael <b>not completely cut off</b>; Esau not either</li><li class=\"d1\">but still a <b>singular line</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1137.28,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 12:8-9: Bethel and Ai, altar number two","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.8: moves to the <b>mountain east of Bethel</b>; tent with <b>Bethel west, Ai east</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Builds an altar - <b>number two</b> - <b>calls on the name of the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v.9: keeps journeying <b>south</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Place names</li><li class=\"d1\">literalists: <b>every word Moses, nothing updated</b> (from the intro)</li><li class=\"d1\">first example: <b>Jacob names Bethel</b> - Abraham's <b>unborn grandson</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ai</b> called that when <b>Joshua</b> showed up, not Abraham's time</li><li class=\"d1\">\"even crazier one\" in a couple chapters</li><li class=\"d0\">Common through the Torah: <b>names updated</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes explicit: \"this city, <b>which used to be called</b>...\" - nobody remembers the old name</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes just the <b>new name</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1248.74,"lv":3,"h":"Nomadic in a land already occupied","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Abraham in the promised land but <b>can't settle</b> - people there</li><li class=\"d0\">Lives <b>nomadically</b>, place to place</li><li class=\"d0\">Also <b>flocks and herds</b> - will matter as we go on</li><li class=\"d1\">kept in one place too long → <b>overgraze</b>, food won't grow back</li><li class=\"d1\">so must keep moving; <b>south within Palestine / the Levant</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1304.19,"lv":2,"h":"12:10: famine, down to Egypt","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.10: <b>famine in the land</b>; Abram goes down to <b>Egypt</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Beginning of a pattern</li><li class=\"d1\">why did <b>Joseph</b> bring his father and brothers to Egypt? <b>Famine</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"all of this has happened before, and all of this will happen again\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Abram = founding father, <b>prototype</b> - \"protocapitulate... am I forming a new word?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>images in advance</b> what happens to his descendants</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis-Exodus relationship</b> key to how the <b>Gospels</b> (and rest of NT) say Christ <b>fulfills</b> the OT</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Matthew = exhibit A</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ fulfilling what happened to Israel, \"often in a better way\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1430.12,"lv":3,"h":"Christ and Israel's pattern","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sometimes a <b>contrast</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>40 days and nights</b> in the wilderness, tested like Israel's <b>40 years</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>passes</b>; Israel mostly failed - that generation wiped out, <b>other than Joshua and Caleb</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Out of Egypt I have called my son\"</b> (Matthew)</li><li class=\"d1\">critics: \"ridiculous, that's Israel coming out of Egypt\" → \"yes, <b>exactly what it's talking about</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">famine → God brings Israel into Egypt to <b>save</b> them; then out, back to the <b>land</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Herod slaughtering innocents (<b>connection to Pharaoh</b>); angel brings Christ and family to Egypt, then back to the land</li><li class=\"d1\">pattern <b>\"filled over full\"</b> in Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":1527.69,"lv":3,"h":"Immanuel and the same pattern","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why you don't have to argue <b><i>parthenos</i> vs. <i>alma</i></b> - virgin or young woman?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Isaiah</b> in the <b>Syro-Ephraimite War</b> - poorly named: not Syrians vs. Ephraimites</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Syrians + northern kingdom (Ephraim main tribe)</b> against <b>Judah</b>, besieging <b>Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">king asks Isaiah for a word; sign: <b>young woman will have a baby</b>; before he's old enough to know <b>good and evil</b> → delivered from enemies</li><li class=\"d0\">Matthew: \"remember that?\" - when the baby's born, people know <b>deliverance is near</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fuller way: Jesus born, <b>shepherds</b> see it, salvation close at hand</li><li class=\"d0\">Arguing \"not the same word\" or \"they are\" - <b>waste of time</b>; not what Matthew is doing</li><li class=\"d0\">Patterns <b>first sketched in Genesis</b> (Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph) → partly fulfilled in Torah → <b>final fulfillment in Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"rough sketch stage\"; famine in Canaan → \"right to Egypt to wait this out\"; \"Spoilers\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1711.24,"lv":2,"h":"12:11-13: \"Say you are my sister\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.11-13: near Egypt, Abram to Sarai: <b>\"you are a beautiful woman\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Egyptians will say \"his wife\" → <b>kill me, let you live</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"say you are my <b>sister</b>, that it may be well with me for your sake\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Going back to <b>civilization</b> - <b>Cain builds the first city</b> not many chapters ago</li><li class=\"d0\">His wife is <b>like 70</b> - \"super good looking woman\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you know how those Egyptians are\" → off the husband, take the wife</li></ul>"},{"t":1802.45,"lv":2,"h":"What's wrong with the plan","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Problem 1: trust</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul uses Abraham as <b>benchmark of faithfulness</b>, loyalty, trust</li><li class=\"d1\">one big show: \"get up and go... not even telling you where\" - he went</li><li class=\"d1\">now: \"what happened to the <b>whole trust God thing</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">thinks he'll be killed <b>randomly</b>; looks out for himself by <b>lying and treachery</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Problem 2: Sarai</b></li><li class=\"d1\">did something about <b>his own</b> not getting killed - nothing about someone <b>having relations with his wife</b></li><li class=\"d1\">someone might value that <b>more than their own life</b> - \"not Abram at this point\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Dynamic in the story: not only <b>Abram's trust in God</b> but <b>how he treats Sarai / Sarah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">name change later; becomes important with <b>Hagar</b> - an undercurrent</li></ul>"},{"t":1928.21,"lv":2,"h":"Not a \"modern feminist\" reading","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Already in <b>Genesis 1</b>: male and female <b>both human</b>, equally in the <b>image of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">major thing in the ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle</b> (4th c. BC): women = <b>deformed men</b>, lower body temperature</li><li class=\"d1\">his system: beautiful, <b>internally consistent, almost entirely wrong</b>; \"did not get how women's bodies work\"</li><li class=\"d1\">yet a \"major breakthrough\" - <b>Hippocratic corpus</b> thought women a <b>different species</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"he was the <b>feminist of his day</b>\" - centuries later</li><li class=\"d0\">Bible on a <b>different page</b>: <b>Genesis 2</b>, creation of Eve, how <b>marriage</b> is spoken of - groundwork</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham-Sarah, later <b>Isaac and Rebecca</b> read in that light</li><li class=\"d0\">Not good husbandry: <b>\"someone's gonna sleep with my wife either way, I just don't want to get killed\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Adam standing there</b> while she ate - \"she didn't drop dead, I guess I'll try it\" - don't do that</li></ul>"},{"t":2117.27,"lv":2,"h":"12:14-16: Sarai taken to Pharaoh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.14: Egyptians <b>\"saw the woman\"</b>, very beautiful</li><li class=\"d1\">echoes <b>Genesis 2-3</b>: Eve not named Eve until after the expulsion, after Cain - before that <b>\"woman\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">language like Eve <b>looking at the fruit</b> (\"not apple\") - saw it was <b>beautiful</b>, looked good to eat</li><li class=\"d1\">at 70 - \"impressive\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.15: <b>Pharaoh's princes</b> commend her; she's taken to <b>Pharaoh's house</b></li><li class=\"d1\">another woman for the <b>harem</b>; kings had many wives and concubines</li><li class=\"d1\">\"concubine\" = <b>polite word</b> for sex life (Victorian English)</li><li class=\"d0\">v.16: Pharaoh treats Abram well - <b>sheep, oxen, donkeys, servants, mules, camels</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if brother and sister: Pharaoh marries your sister → <b>you're an in-law</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nothing about Abram being <b>mad</b>, deeply upset, wanting to correct it</li></ul>"},{"t":2275.35,"lv":2,"h":"12:17: the Lord plagues Pharaoh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.17: <b>Lord plagued Pharaoh and his house</b> because of <b>Sarai, Abram's wife</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why? Early in the chapter: <b>\"he who blesses you I will bless, he who curses you I will curse\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Abram caring or not, Pharaoh doing evil to Abram</li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>protects Sarai even though Abram isn't</b> and should be</li><li class=\"d0\">Israel later described as <b>bride / wife of God</b> - outlines of the pattern again</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pre-pattern</b> of the broader pattern in the Torah → fullness in <b>Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2335.47,"lv":2,"h":"Heroes who grow","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Biblical figures show <b>growth over their life</b> - learn, come to understand, over relationship with God</li><li class=\"d0\">Scripture <b>not scared to say negative things</b> about \"heroes\"</li><li class=\"d1\">repentance and learning is what is <b>modeled to us</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>knows our frame, remembers we are dust</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not always perfect, heroic, virtuous</li><li class=\"d1\">wants <b>repentance when we do wrong, transformation, growth, drawing closer</b></li><li class=\"d0\">People shown to emulate are people who <b>did that</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Early - \"second Bible study\" - not many verses into Abram's story</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>contrast</b>: big thing (leave everything, go to parts unknown) → <b>faithful, trusted</b></li><li class=\"d1\">personal thing: <b>his own life</b> → waffled</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>protecting and treating his wife right</b> → \"he blew it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but this is <b>where he's starting out</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2515.78,"lv":2,"h":"12:18-20: Pharaoh rebukes Abram","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">After the plagues Pharaoh <b>calls Abram</b>: <b>\"What is this you have done to me?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Why did you not tell me she was your wife? Why say <b>she is my sister</b>? I might have taken her... <b>Here is your wife. Take her and go</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I might have taken her as my wife\" = <b>consummating</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in the ancient world and into medieval period: <b>consummation</b> is when a marriage becomes a marriage</li><li class=\"d1\">he hadn't, but might have → plagues <b>worse</b>, <b>adultery</b> with another man's wife</li><li class=\"d0\">So he has to <b>correct, challenge, call Abram to repentance</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2586.59,"lv":2,"h":"This Pharaoh and <i>ma'at</i>","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">This Pharaoh, <b>unlike the Pharaoh of the Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">looks at the plagues, sees something wrong, <b>figures out what</b>, tries to make it right</li><li class=\"d0\">Pharaoh's job per the Egyptians: maintain <b><i>ma'at</i></b> = <b>justice</b>, everything in its place, in good order</li><li class=\"d1\">his sense of <i>ma'at</i> is in line with <b>God's sense of justice</b> - why he's accurate</li><li class=\"d1\">Abram <b>corrected by Pharaoh</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Anti-type</b> for the Pharaoh of the Exodus</li><li class=\"d1\">receives plagues but <b>unwilling / unable / refuses</b> to act as a righteous, just king</li></ul>"},{"t":2657.6,"lv":3,"h":"12:20: sent away with everything","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.20: Pharaoh commands his men; they send Abram away <b>with his wife and all he had</b></li><li class=\"d1\">keeps everything from Pharaoh's good treatment <b>for Sarai's sake</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Again the Exodus pattern: descendants <b>pillage the Egyptians</b> on the way out</li><li class=\"d1\">leaves with all he had <b>plus more</b>, after the plagues</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pattern complete</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Abram at the <b>beginning of his journey, life, growth, faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">rest of his story as he <b>becomes Abraham</b>: learn, grow, change in ongoing <b>walking with God</b></li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>Genesis 13:1</b>"});
